@@s001 Code: fast input and output in Java
@@s002 Code: fast input and output in Java
@@s003 The Kattis program template: BufferedReader, StringTokenizer, StringBuilder. Every line explained, and the syntax notes you need.
@@s004 The starting template
@@s005 Almost every Kattis problem has this form: read the number of cases, read the lines, compute, print.
@@s006 The starting template
@@s007 <code>BufferedReader</code> wraps <code>InputStreamReader</code>, which in turn wraps <code>System.in</code>. They are layers: bytes, characters, lines.
@@s008 <code>readLine()</code> reads a line; <code>StringTokenizer</code> splits it into tokens separated by spaces.
@@s009 I accumulate the output in memory: printing line by line is slow with large outputs.
@@s010 <code>nextToken()</code> returns the token as text; <code>parseInt</code> converts it to an <code>int</code>.
@@s011 <code>append</code> concatenates; the character <code>'\n'</code> starts a new line.
@@s012 A single print at the end, with all the output.
@@s013 Syntax notes
@@s014 <code>import java.io.*;</code> and <code>import java.util.*;</code>: the asterisk imports all the classes of the package.
@@s015 <code>throws IOException</code>: <code>readLine()</code> can fail, and Java requires you to declare it.
@@s016 The name of the public class must match the file name: here <code>Main</code>.
@@s017 Running it
@@s018 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
